fuxi: 0101 -- retire the SM7150 reference board config and its dual define

ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET is now ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_FUXI at every one of the
167 C-level checks and at the make level, so nothing reads the old board any
more and the second -D in MACHINE_FLAGS_ARM64_FUXI is dead weight.

Removing it alone would leave two traps:

  * `#define SWEET 1` in FUXI.h. With it in place a missed `#if SWEET` still
    evaluates true, so the build would look fine even if a driver guard had
    been missed. Removing it turns that class of mistake into a leak of
    compiled-out code, which CI can see.
  * config/MASTER.arm64.sweet + pexpert/pexpert/arm64/SWEET.h + the
    MACHINE_FLAGS_ARM64_SWEET entry: the SM7150 reference port. Its file lists
    name the renamed sources and its board header defines the SM7150 GIC base
    and 19/1 PPI numbering. Left in place, `MACHINE_CONFIG=SWEET` would still
    "build" a kernel for the wrong SoC. Retired instead, so asking for it fails
    loudly rather than producing a silently wrong kernel.

The board_config.h block it leaves behind is `#if defined(X) && !defined(X)`
after the gate rename -- dead code that includes a header this patch deletes,
so it goes too.

Nothing here changes the FUXI kernel: the value of MAX_CPU_CLUSTERS (8),
MAX_CPUS (8) and every driver gate come from the FUXI branch that already
existed and was already the one taken.

diff --git a/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/board_config.h b/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/board_config.h
